#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065360 :tutorial/content "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1$. For example,\n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1$. First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{4} &= 16 \\\\\n2^{3} &= 8 \\\\\n2^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n2^{1} &= 2 \\\\\n2^{0} &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "73efd6db-4f12-5b29-bb69-8db83970c328" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3087 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
